f5"},"finalFen":"6k1/1p4p1/p4p2/5N1R/2p4Q/P1P5/1PK1rP1P/6q1 w - - 3 37"},"white":"SeanWinshand","black":"ShaakSiv","labels":["book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","book","excellent","best","best","excellent","best","best","best","excellent","best","excellent","best","excellent","best","excellent","best","good","inaccuracy","inaccuracy","best","best","inaccuracy","inaccuracy","good","best","good","good","best","best","best","mistake","inaccuracy","best","blunder","great","best","best","forced","best","best","excellent","best","brilliant","best","best","forced","brilliant","mistake","best","best","best"],"moments":[{"ply":52,"name":"26…e5","cls":"mistake","color":"b","headline":"26…e5 is a mistake: Black's winning chances go from 43% to 24%.","points":["White answers 27.fxe5, which takes the pawn on e5.","After fxe5, White wins 1 point of material within 2 moves.","Better was 26…g6 (+0.76): makes an escape square for the king, so back-rank checks lose their sting."],"better":"26…g6 makes luft for the king and takes h5 away from the queen. Engine line: 27.Re5 Qf8 28.Rbe1 Bc6 29.Qg4 Qg7 (+0.76)."},{"ply":55,"name":"28.Rh5","cls":"blunder","color":"w","headline":"28.Rh5 is a blunder: White's winning chances go from 70% to 13%.","points":["Black answers 28…Qg6+: the queen forks the king and the rook: both can't be saved at once.","After Qg6+, Black wins 5 points of material within 4 moves.","Better was 28.Rbe1 (+2.25): the rook takes the open e-file."],"better":"28.Rbe1 stops …Qg6+. After 28…Re8 29.Qg5 Qf8 30.R1e3 Rxe5 31.fxe5 Qd8, the rooks are traded (+2.25)."},{"ply":56,"name":"28…Qg6+","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"28…Qg6+! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The queen forks the king and the rook: both can't be saved at once.","The queen joins the attack: it now hits 2 squares around the White king.","The next best move, 28…f5, would drop Black's winning chances from 87% to 7% (+6.84).","28…Qg6+ wins a rook, picking it up with 29…Qxb1+.","Sets up a sequence and wins a rook once the captures settle, picking it up with 29…Qxb1+.","White answers 29.Kf1."]},{"ply":64,"name":"32…Bxf5","cls":"brilliant","color":"b","headline":"32…Bxf5!! A real sacrifice: Black gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the bishop on f5 where it can be taken.","White can take, but after 33.Nxf5 Rd1+ 34.Ke2 Re8+ the engine rates it −5.01.","Takes the pawn on f5.","Protects the queen on g6, which was hanging.","32…Bxf5 stops the mate threat fxg6.","White was threatening fxg6, with mate in 5. Bxf5 takes care of it."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"32…Bxf5!! offers the bishop on f5. Taking it gives Black an attack worth more than the bishop."},{"title":"What Black gives up","text":"32…Bxf5 puts the bishop on f5 where it can be taken by the knight on d4 and the rook on h5. That's a bishop on offer."},{"title":"If White takes the bishop","text":"33.Nxf5 is met by 33…Rd1+. Then 34…Re8+ gives check, and 35…Rd3+ gives check. Black is still two pawns down, but the attack is worth more: Black is winning (−4.52). This is White's best try."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 32…Qxh5, is clearly weaker (+0.53 against −5.01). White answers 33.Qxh5, taking the queen on h5. That would leave Black a little worse."}]},{"ply":68,"name":"34…Re8+","cls":"brilliant","color":"b","headline":"34…Re8+!! A real sacrifice: Black gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Leaves the rook on d1 where it can be taken.","Taking is too dangerous: White's best reply is 35.Kf3 Rd3+ 36.Ne3 b5 instead (the engine rates it −4.51).","The rook takes the open e-file, aiming at the king.","The rook joins the attack: it now hits 2 squares around the White king.","34…Re8+ takes the open e-file, aiming at the king, and joins the attack on the king.","Gives check, so the reply is forced."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"34…Re8+!! leaves the rook on d1 hanging. Taking it leads to mate."},{"title":"What Black gives up","text":"The rook on d1 is attacked by the king, and 34…Re8+ doesn't save it. That's a whole rook on offer."},{"title":"If White takes the rook","text":"35.Kxd1 is met by 35…Qg1+. Then 36…Qe1+ gives check, and 37…Re2# is mate."},{"title":"White's best try","text":"White's best is to leave the rook alone and play 35.Kf3. Black answers 35…Rd3+. Then 37…Kf7 threatens …Qf5+, winning a knight, and 38…Kxe8 takes the rook on e8. Material is level and Black is winning (−4.51)."},{"title":"Why not save the rook first?","text":"Saving it with 34…Rd7, the best way to do that, is clearly weaker (0.00 against −4.51). White answers 35.Rh8+. That would leave Black level."}]},{"ply":69,"name":"35.Kxd1","cls":"mistake","color":"w","headline":"35.Kxd1 is a mistake: White's winning chances go from 16% to 0%. It allows mate in 3.","points":["Black answers 35…Qg1+: the queen joins the attack: it now hits 2 squares around the White king.","Better was 35.Kf3 (−4.51): the king steps out of check to f3."],"better":"35.Kf3 threatens Rh8+, with mate in 5. After 35…Rd3+ 36.Ne3 b5 37.Rh8+ Kf7 38.Rxe8 Kxe8 39.Qh8+, the rooks are traded (−4.51)."}],"opening":{"name":"Horwitz Defense","eco":"A40","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 11…Bd7: 22 moves in all, 11 by White and 11 by Black.","Theory runs out after 11…Bd7: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}